// Orisha in a unikernel — the whole server, no operating system.
//
// Nothing in this file is unikernel-specific. The handler and `serve` are the
// same two lines a hosted Orisha program writes; what changes is one build
// flag, which selects a different `~proc run|…` inside orisha/pump and nothing
// else. That is the pump seam doing its job.
//
//   koruc main.kz --build=unikraft
//   zig build-lib wrapper.zig -target x86_64-freestanding \
//       -O ReleaseSmall -fno-stack-protector -femit-bin=libkoruapp.a
//   UK_CFLAGS="-std=gnu17" kraft build --arch x86_64 --plat qemu --no-prompt
//   qemu-system-x86_64 -kernel .unikraft/build/orisha_qemu-x86_64 \
//     -cpu 'qemu64,+pdpe1gb,+rdrand,+rdseed,-vmx,-svm' \
//     -m 64M -nographic -no-reboot -display none -parallel none \
//     -netdev user,id=n0,hostfwd=tcp::18335-:8080 \
//     -device virtio-net-pci,netdev=n0
//
// Measured 2026-08-09, three requests over the host-forwarded port:
//
//   HTTP/1.1 200 OK
//   Content-Length: 39
//   Content-Type: text/plain
//   Date: Sun, 09 Aug 2026 14:52:40 GMT
//   Connection: keep-alive
//
//   Hello from Orisha, inside a unikernel.
//
// Two things had to leave lib/index.kz before this could link, and both were
// Orisha's own, not the compiler's and not Unikraft's:
//
//   `@cImport(@cInclude("time.h"))` for the Date header — replaced by one
//   `extern fn time`, with the calendar arithmetic written out. A freestanding
//   target has no libc HEADERS; it does have the symbol, at link time.
//
//   `std.heap.page_allocator` on every Request — that is an OS allocator and
//   reaches std.posix for mmap. It is now a seam: hosted keeps the page
//   allocator, freestanding gets a fixed arena in the image, reset per
//   exchange because a pump delivers one at a time.
//
// The handler is written as a record return. A `~proc orisha:handler|zig`
// implementation compiles and links and then does NOT attach — the server
// comes up and answers with Orisha's built-in 404 instead. Measured the same
// day on both targets; it is a real defect and it is not this example's.

~import orisha

// The handler is implemented with a proc. `response { … }` used to be the
// spelling the library's docs showed; it was removed from the language on
// 2026-08-09 (koru 050b3549) because a single outcome does not get a name, so
// there is nothing to prefer this over any more — a handler returns the record.
~orisha:handler -> { status: 200, body: "Hello from Orisha, inside a unikernel.\n", content_type: "text/plain" }

~orisha:serve(port: 8080)
| shutdown _ |> _
| failed _ |> _
